  </widgets>
  <info>## WHAT IS IT?

A spatial agent-based model of formal and informal electricity access. This working revision corrects provider switching, neighborhood caches and weekly load accounting, and adds opt-in decision logging. The original model and its results are retained under results/base/.

## HOW IT WORKS

Households have fixed income/demand, a monthly spending reference, and Boolean attitudes to KPLC and informal suppliers. Issue-triggered switching uses a logistic probability combining relative monthly cost savings, the alternative-minus-current attitude difference, and switching inertia. The three choice weights are uncalibrated starting assumptions.

Each week pole-demand records cash- and connection-constrained requested energy before outages. pole-usage records delivered energy. Both are recomputed each week; overload is determined from current requested load. Direct informal drops and hook-ons share the same weekly cap. Cached neighbors are initialized after all households are positioned.

## HOW TO USE IT

Run setup and go. The default profile is experiments/base.json. To record decisions after setup, run decision-log-start "decisions.csv"; call decision-log-stop when finished. Existing log files are protected from overwrite. scripts/run_model_diagnostics.py supplies headless weekly results and complete logs.

## INTERPRETATION

Attitudes remain Boolean and follow the original learning rules. The income/budget formulation and informal-only unconnected entry rule are retained. Logged co-occurring issue labels are not causal effect estimates. See docs/model-revision.md for the formula, changes, tests and original-model compatibility.
</info>
